    inbox. Spec A gets written with Spec B's constraints baked in — every mutation records an actor, every mutation path
    is funnelled through a single service layer so there's exactly one place to emit events from. Slowest to first
    notification, most solid.
  - [1mOne combined spec, phased plan.[0m Everything in a single design doc, with the plan ordering foundation before
    notifications. Keeps the notification requirements visibly driving the foundation's shape. Risk: a large spec, and
    large specs are where ambiguity hides.
  - [1mMinimal substrate, notifications early.[0m Deliberately thin foundation — seeded users and a simple session login
    instead of real registration/password-reset/email-verification, tasks with just title/status/assignee, comments.
    Enough for notifications to be real and testable end to end, with auth hardening as explicit later work. Fastest
    path to the thing you actually asked for.

  The tradeoff is honesty about auth: option 3 ships something demonstrably working much sooner, but "seeded users,
  simple sessions" must not quietly become production auth. If you pick it, that limitation goes in the spec as a named
  constraint with the hardening work written down, not as a TODO we forget.

  My recommendation: [1moption 3.[0m Your stated goal is notifications, and real user registration and password recovery are a
  well-understood problem that doesn't teach us anything about the design you actually care about. Thin the part that's
  solved, spend the design budget on the part that isn't.

  Alongside that, the tooling question — cheapest to decide now, before any code exists.

